WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.950
SPEAKER: One thing
that makes Google Cloud

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:04.080
production ready
and ops-friendly

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.910
is the broad range
of management,

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:09.730
monitoring, and alerting
tools that it comes with.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:12.310
While tools are no
magic wand and do not

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:15.490
replace DevOps or
SRE practices, you

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:17.982
should really watch this
video to better understand

00:00:17.982 --> 00:00:18.940
what they're all about.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:22.764
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:29.150
Now, before I start talking
about specific tools,

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.400
let me mention that
these products work

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:35.000
best in the context of a
team applying SRE or Site

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.970
Reliability
Engineering principles.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.350
There is not enough
time to delve into this,

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:44.030
but you'll find links to SRE
literature on Service-Level

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.360
Objectives, or
SLOs, error budget,

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.170
and blameless postmortems in
the video description below.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.590
The Hello World for DevOps
tools is arguably the ability

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:58.610
to collect, read, and parse
logs across a distributed

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.520
infrastructure involving
multiple products.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.540
Cloud Logging stores logs
across all Google Cloud products

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:08.240
and provides you with
search, monitoring,

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.680
and alerting capabilities.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.910
It also comes with an API
to ingest custom log data

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:15.500
from any source.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.750
Because it is a fully
managed service,

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.790
there are no hard
drives to provision

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:21.850
or partitions to resize.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:25.190
And it can ingest application
and system log data

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:28.370
from thousands of
sources simultaneously.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:31.640
Even better, you can
analyze all the log data

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.770
as it arrives in real
time without having

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:37.040
to worry about synchronizing
your server pods

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.870
or managing time zones.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:41.270
Logs are made up
of entries created

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.690
by Google Cloud Services,
third-party applications,

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:46.760
or by your own code.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:51.020
The message carried by the log
entry is called the payload,

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.780
and it can be a simple
string or structured data.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.980
Examples of log
entries can include

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:01.770
details of a compute engine
instance starting up,

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.580
a new file being
uploaded to a bucket,

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.920
a call made to a
machine learning API,

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.259
or anything that your
application writes

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:11.900
to the standard
or error outputs.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.060
Each log entry
indicates where it

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.900
came from by including the
name of the monitored resource.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.980
Using the console's Logs Viewer,
you can query logging data

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.800
and obtain a precise
subset of all the log

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.850
entries in your project.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.340
The query will let
you find entries

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.940
for a given resource from
different namespaces based

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.350
on the log level and,
of course, by timestamp.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:40.140
Note that if using the
console isn't your thing,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.780
these queries are also
accessible from the logging

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.820
API, as well as from
the command line.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:49.170
Storing logs is free for
the first gigabyte for every

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:53.640
project and then comes at
$0.50 per additional gigabyte.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.260
And your alerting
policy can be set up

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.470
to trigger when your monthly
logs ingested exceeds a given

00:02:58.470 --> 00:02:59.610
limit.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.920
But you can also
define excluded logs

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.250
so that some are not
stored in the first place.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.280
The logs viewer and the
logs API are powerful tools

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.410
on their own, but
you can also decide

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:14.220
to export your logs
to cloud storage,

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:18.300
to BigQuery, or to
Pub/Sub, which then in turn

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.970
redirects them to virtually
any log storage system.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:24.860
Exporting logs is
likely something

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:29.250
that you will want for archival
or potentially legal purposes,

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.780
but also maybe to benefit
from more advanced analytics.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.700
There is even more to
logs, but hopefully this

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.280
gives you a good sense of
what every Google Cloud

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.970
product can rely on.

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.320
So now you know how logs are
aggregated across all of Google

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:45.470
Cloud and how to navigate them.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:48.400
If you liked this video, please
like, subscribe, comment,

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.890
share, and look forward to more
Google Cloud Essentials videos.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:55.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

